with nq as (
  insert into public.questions (stage, question_type, stem, model_answer, explanation, key_points, subject_id, topic_id, paper_id, difficulty, marks,
                                estimated_seconds, origin, status, parent_question_id, variation_type, ai_review, passage_group, passage_position, verified_by, subject_group_id, specialisation_id, image_url)
  values ('ME', 'long_answer', 'a) Ugyen Guru Rinpoche identified regions and mountain valleys in the Himalayas as sacred Beyul for Buddhism, and tradition maintains that Bhutan is among the most sacred and sanctified. What does Beyul mean to you and what explanations can you provide to affirm that Bhutan is the ''Auspicious Beyul''? Please justify. (10 marks)', 'a) A beyul is a hidden, sacred land blessed by Guru Rinpoche, where he concealed treasures (terma) and which he prophesied would be a refuge for spiritual practice in difficult times. To me, a beyul means a place where nature, people and the Dharma live in harmony.
Bhutan can be called an auspicious beyul because: (1) Guru Rinpoche visited Bhutan in the 8th century and meditated at many sites, such as Paro Taktsang, Kurjey in Bumthang and Singye Dzong in Lhuentse, leaving his body imprint and blessings; (2) several places, such as Khenpajong in Lhuentse, are traditionally named as beyul; (3) great tertons such as Pema Lingpa revealed treasures here, as prophesied; (4) Bhutan was known as Lhomon and Menjong, the southern land of medicinal herbs; (5) its pristine forests and mountains, still largely protected, and its living Buddhist culture and peace make it a true sanctuary.', null, '[{"point": "Meaning: beyul = hidden sacred land blessed by Guru Rinpoche as a refuge for practice in degenerate times, with treasures (terma) concealed", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Evidence for Bhutan: Guru Rinpoche''s visits and meditation sites (Taktsang, Kurjey in Bumthang, Singye Dzong in Lhuentse), named beyul such as Khenpajong; terma revealed by tertons (Pema Lingpa); names Lhomon/ Menjong (land of medicinal herbs); pristine environment, living Buddhist culture, peace", "marks": 7}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'history-beyul-and-zhabdrung-s-succession'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (General): BCSE 2021, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2021 Main Examination, General category, Paper III", "year": 2021, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-general-3-2021b2-a1', 1, null, null, null, null)
  returning id
)
