with nq as (
  insert into public.questions (stage, question_type, stem, model_answer, explanation, key_points, subject_id, topic_id, paper_id, difficulty, marks,
                                estimated_seconds, origin, status, parent_question_id, variation_type, ai_review, passage_group, passage_position, verified_by, subject_group_id, specialisation_id, image_url)
  values ('ME', 'long_answer', 'b) At the age of fifty-eight, Zhabdrung Rinpoche (1594-1651) decided to retire from state affairs and enter into final retreat. He probably passed away not long after his entry into retreat, but his death was kept a state secret for fifty-eight years till the year 1708. In your opinion, what could be the plausible reasons for keeping his death a state secret? Explain with specific reference to the country''s security and the problem of Zhabdrung''s succession. (10 marks)', 'b) Plausible reasons for keeping Zhabdrung''s death secret:
Security: the new state that Zhabdrung had unified was still young, and he was the single figure holding together different valleys and leaders. Bhutan faced repeated invasions from Tibet and opposition from rival lamas. Announcing his death could have encouraged Tibet to attack and local chiefs to rebel, so the leaders announced that he was in strict retreat and continued to issue orders in his name.
Succession: Zhabdrung had no clear successor able to take on both spiritual and temporal authority - his son Jampal Dorji was not able to rule. The state needed time to settle who would lead and to recognise his reincarnation(s). Meanwhile the Desi governed civil affairs and the Je Khenpo religious affairs in his name, preserving the dual system. Only after incarnations had been recognised and the system was secure could the death be revealed (around 1705-1708).', null, '[{"point": "Security: the young state still being consolidated; Zhabdrung''s charisma held it together; threat of Tibetan invasions and internal rivals; news of death could invite attack and rebellion", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Succession: no clear successor - his son Jampal Dorji was not able to rule; need to wait for/ recognise reincarnations; rule continued in his name through Desi and Je Khenpo; secret ended when succession could be arranged (incarnations recognised)", "marks": 5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'history-beyul-and-zhabdrung-s-succession'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (General): BCSE 2021, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2021 Main Examination, General category, Paper III", "year": 2021, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-general-3-2021b2-a1', 2, null, null, null, null)
  returning id
)
