with nq as (
  insert into public.questions (stage, question_type, stem, model_answer, explanation, key_points, subject_id, topic_id, paper_id, difficulty, marks,
                                estimated_seconds, origin, status, parent_question_id, variation_type, ai_review, passage_group, passage_position, verified_by, subject_group_id, specialisation_id, image_url)
  values ('ME', 'long_answer', 'a) Among all the dzongs of Bhutan, Punakha Dzong is spiritually, culturally and politically the most important dzong in the history of Bhutan. In this regard, discuss the importance of Punakha Dzong with specific reference to its religious and political significance to the Bhutanese people. (10 marks)', 'a) Punakha Dzong (Pungthang Dewachenpoi Phodrang, ''palace of great happiness'') was built by Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyel in 1637-38 at the confluence of the Pho Chhu and Mo Chhu.
Religious significance: its Machen Lhakhang holds the sacred embalmed remains of Zhabdrung and the self-created image Rangjung Kharsapani, the most sacred relics of the nation; it is the winter residence of the Je Khenpo and the Zhung Dratshang; and the Punakha Drubchen, which commemorates victory over Tibetan invaders, and the Punakha Tshechu are held here.
Political significance: it served as the capital and seat of government until the capital moved to Thimphu in 1955; Ugyen Wangchuck was enthroned here as the first King on 17 December 1907; the Treaty of Punakha with British India was signed here in 1910; and it hosts important royal ceremonies, such as the Royal Wedding of 2011. It remains a powerful symbol of national unity and history.', null, '[{"point": "Religious: built by Zhabdrung 1637-38 (Pungthang Dewachenpoi Phodrang); Machen Lhakhang holds Zhabdrung''s sacred remains and Rangjung Kharsapani; winter residence of the Je Khenpo and Zhung Dratshang; Punakha Drubchen and Tshechu", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Political: capital and seat of government until 1955; enthronement of the First King on 17 December 1907; treaty of Punakha 1910; coronations/ royal events (e.g. Royal Wedding 2011); symbol of unity", "marks": 5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'dzongs-and-democracy'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (General): BCSE 2021, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2021 Main Examination, General category, Paper III", "year": 2021, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-general-3-2021b2-a3', 1, null, null, null, null)
  returning id
