  values ('ME', 'long_answer', 'b) Article 2 (The Institution of Monarchy), clause 1 of the Constitution of the Kingdom of Bhutan states that "His Majesty the Druk Gyalpo is the Head of State and the symbol of unity of the Kingdom and of the people of Bhutan". Explain the role and importance of monarchy in strengthening and promoting the institution of democracy in Bhutan. (10 marks)', 'b) Bhutan''s democracy was a gift and initiative from the throne: His Majesty the Fourth King devolved executive powers in 1998, initiated the drafting of the Constitution, and introduced parliamentary democracy in 2008. As Head of State and symbol of unity, the King remains above party politics, so that people stay united even when elections divide them. The King is the guardian of the Constitution, appoints key constitutional office holders on the advice of bodies such as the National Judicial Commission, and ensures that institutions such as the ECB, ACC, judiciary and RCSC remain independent. Through kidu, De-suung and Gyalsung, the King reaches every citizen and builds a culture of service. His Majesty''s addresses guide elected leaders and remind citizens of their responsibilities. Constitutional safeguards - retirement at 65 and a procedure for abdication - show that the monarchy itself is placed within the democratic framework. Thus the monarchy strengthens and protects democracy in Bhutan.', null, '[{"point": "Role: Fourth King introduced democracy and the Constitution from the throne; King as Head of State and symbol of unity above party politics; guardian of the Constitution; appoints constitutional office holders on advice; kidu and national service; guidance to elected leaders and citizens; safeguards (retirement at 65, abdication procedure)", "marks": 10}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
