  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'Since 1961, socio-economic development activities have been planned and implemented based on a Five Year Plan (FYP) cycle. Currently, we are in the middle of the 12th FYP and the preparatory work for the 13th FYP is nowhere in sight. There are talks going around that the current Government plans to discontinue the practice of undertaking socio-economic development based on the FYP cycle.
Do you agree with the Government''s plan of discontinuing the practice of undertaking socio-economic development based on the FYP cycle? Justify. (20 marks)', 'Background: since the First Five Year Plan (1961-66), fully funded by India, Bhutan has planned its development in five-year cycles. These plans built roads, schools, hospitals, hydropower and institutions, and from the 9th Plan they were guided by GNH; the 12th FYP ran from 2018 to 2023.
Arguments for keeping the FYP cycle: (1) it gives a clear, predictable framework for all agencies and local governments; (2) it allows coordination and a balanced allocation of resources across sectors and dzongkhags; (3) development partners align their support with the Plan through the Round Table Meeting; (4) it provides targets for monitoring, evaluation and accountability; (5) it gives continuity across changes of government; and (6) gewog and dzongkhag plans give people a voice in planning.
Arguments for change: (1) a rigid five-year cycle may not match the election cycle and may change with each government; (2) it is slow to respond to shocks such as COVID-19; (3) long-term transformation (e.g. economic diversification, climate change) needs a longer vision; and (4) plans may focus on spending rather than results.
My view: I do not agree with completely discontinuing planning in cycles. Bhutan needs a planning framework, but it should be reformed - a long-term vision with flexible, rolling medium-term plans, focused on results and able to adjust to shocks. In fact, the 13th FYP (2024-2029) was eventually retained, placed within a 10-year strategic framework, which combines the strengths of both approaches.', null, '[{"point": "Background: FYPs since 1961 (1st FYP funded by India), achievements in infrastructure, health, education; GNH-based plans; 12th FYP 2018-2023", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Arguments for keeping FYPs: predictable framework, coordination of agencies and local governments, resource mobilisation and RTM with partners, monitoring and accountability, continuity across governments, decentralised planning (gewog/ dzongkhag plans)", "marks": 6}, {"point": "Arguments for change: rigid five-year cycle ties to election cycle, slow to respond to shocks (COVID-19), need for longer-term vision and flexible rolling plans, results-based and evidence-based planning", "marks": 6}, {"point": "Clear position and justification; note that a 13th FYP (2024-2029) was in the end retained within a 10-year strategic framework", "marks": 4}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'development-planning'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 20,
    2160, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (General): BCSE 2021, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2021 Main Examination, General category, Paper III", "year": 2021, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, null, null, null, null, null, null)
