with nq as (
  insert into public.questions (stage, question_type, stem, model_answer, explanation, key_points, subject_id, topic_id, paper_id, difficulty, marks,
                                estimated_seconds, origin, status, parent_question_id, variation_type, ai_review, passage_group, passage_position, verified_by, subject_group_id, specialisation_id, image_url)
  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'Bhutan is one of the fastest growing economies in South Asia, with an average growth rate of 6.12 percent for the period 2010-2019 (pre-pandemic). The pandemic has, however, disrupted the economy and the growth plummeted to an all-time low of -10.08% in 2020. Further, the economy inherently lacks diversification, which is likely to impact growth in the long run.

[Chart: Growth trend 2010-2020, growth rate (%) - 2010: 11.94, 2011: 7.98, 2012: 5.12, 2013: 2.12, 2014: 5.78, 2015: 6.64, 2016: 8.13, 2017: 4.65, 2018: 3.06, 2019: 5.76, 2020: -10.08. Data source: National Accounts Statistics 2021, National Statistics Bureau.]

Suggest potential sectors/industries apart from hydropower and tourism that will diversify the economy as well as sustain the growth in the long run. (20 marks)', 'The chart shows that Bhutan''s growth has been high but volatile - from 11.94 percent in 2010 down to 2.12 percent in 2013, back up to 8.13 percent in 2016, then 3.06 percent in 2018 and a collapse to -10.08 percent in 2020. Growth has moved with hydropower construction and tourism, which shows the danger of a narrow economic base.
Potential sectors for diversification:
1. Commercial agriculture and agro-processing: high-value and organic products (cardamom, mushrooms, oranges, potatoes, honey, dairy), with processing and branding for domestic and export markets; this also reduces food imports.
2. ICT and digital services: software development, IT-enabled services, data centres using clean energy, fintech and e-commerce - suited to an educated young population and not limited by terrain.
3. Manufacturing and cottage and small industries: wood-based products, furniture, handicrafts and textiles, food and beverages, using local materials.
4. Mining and minerals with value addition: processing dolomite, limestone and other minerals domestically instead of exporting raw materials, under strict environmental controls.
5. Construction materials and industries supplying domestic infrastructure.
6. Services: quality education and health services (including for regional students and patients), financial services.
7. Creative and cultural industries: film, music, crafts, design.
8. Clean energy beyond large hydropower: solar, energy-intensive green industries and carbon credits.
Enablers: skills development, access to finance, foreign direct investment, better infrastructure and logistics, and simpler regulations. A broader base of sectors will make growth more stable and create jobs for youth.', null, '[{"point": "Reads the chart: volatile growth linked to hydropower construction cycles, low growth 2013 and 2018, sharp fall in 2020 from COVID-19; shows risk of a narrow base", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Sectors (with reasons and examples): commercial agriculture and agro-processing/ organic niche products; ICT and digital services; manufacturing and cottage and small industries (wood, handicrafts, food); mining and minerals with value addition; construction materials; financial services; education and health services; creative and cultural industries; renewable energy beyond hydro (solar, green hydrogen) and carbon markets", "marks": 12}, {"point": "Enablers: skills, finance, FDI, infrastructure, regulation; conclusion", "marks": 4}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'economic-diversification'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 20,
    2160, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (General): BCSE 2021, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2021 Main Examination, General category, Paper III", "year": 2021, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, null, null, null, null, null, '/papers/me/gen3-2021-growth-trend.png')
