  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'Bhutan has been successful in containing the spread of a full-blown Covid-19 pandemic under the selfless and benevolent leadership of His Majesty The King. The pandemic has, however, disrupted the economy and adversely affected some of the key economic sectors.
Mention the key economic sectors which are adversely affected by the pandemic. Please elaborate. (20 marks)', 'Key sectors adversely affected:
1. Tourism and hospitality: the hardest hit. With borders closed from March 2020, international arrivals stopped almost completely. Hotels, tour operators, guides, drivers, restaurants and handicraft shops lost their income, thousands lost jobs, and the country lost an important source of convertible currency. Drukair and Bhutan Airlines operated mostly repatriation and essential flights.
2. Construction and hydropower: projects depend on workers and materials from India. Movement restrictions caused labour shortages, delays and cost overruns in hydropower, roads and housing.
3. Manufacturing and mining: supply chains for raw materials and exports were disrupted, and demand fell.
4. Trade, retail and services: lockdowns in 2020 and 2021 closed shops and businesses; small businesses and the informal sector suffered most.
5. Transport: taxis, buses and trucking lost business during restrictions.
6. Financial sector: borrowers could not repay loans; the RMA deferred loan repayments and waived interest, raising risks of non-performing loans.
7. Government finances: revenue fell while spending on health and relief rose, widening the fiscal deficit.
8. Labour market: unemployment rose, and many Bhutanese working abroad returned.
9. Agriculture: marketing and transport of produce were disrupted, although many returnees took up farming.
As a result, the economy contracted by about 10 percent in 2020. The Druk Gyalpo''s Relief Kidu, monetary measures and economic contingency plans helped households and businesses survive.', null, '[{"point": "Tourism and hospitality: border closure from March 2020, hotels, tour operators, guides, drivers, restaurants, handicrafts, airlines - loss of jobs and foreign exchange", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Construction and hydropower: shortage of foreign workers and materials, delays and cost overruns; manufacturing and mining: supply chain disruption, lower demand", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Trade, retail and services: lockdowns, reduced sales, small businesses and informal sector; transport; education; financial sector: loan deferrals, NPL risk; government revenue and fiscal deficit", "marks": 6}, {"point": "Agriculture and labour market: disrupted marketing but some growth in farming; returning workers and unemployment; overall GDP contraction (-10% in 2020); support measures", "marks": 4}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'covid-19-and-the-economy'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 20,
    2160, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
