  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'Bhutan has been experiencing a negative balance of trade for the last many years. In 2020, the trade deficit increased by 6.28 percent against a drop of 34.36 percent in 2019.
Define the term ''Balance of Trade''. Suggest appropriate interventions to minimize the trade deficit. (20 marks)', 'Balance of trade is the difference between the value of a country''s exports and its imports of goods over a given period, usually a year. If exports exceed imports there is a trade surplus; if imports exceed exports there is a trade deficit. It is the largest part of the current account of the balance of payments.
Bhutan has a persistent trade deficit because it imports most of its fuel, food, vehicles, machinery, construction materials and consumer goods, while its exports are concentrated in a few products - electricity, ferro-alloys and other minerals, and some agricultural products - and hydropower construction itself requires large imports.
Interventions to minimise the deficit:
1. Increase exports: complete hydropower projects on time; process minerals domestically to export higher-value products; promote agricultural and processed exports such as cardamom, oranges, potatoes, mushrooms and spring water; find new markets in Bangladesh, Thailand and beyond.
2. Import substitution: achieve greater food self-sufficiency through commercial farming, irrigation and protection from wildlife; support local manufacturing of goods currently imported.
3. Discourage non-essential imports: higher taxes or temporary restrictions on luxury goods and vehicles.
4. Reduce fuel imports: promote electric vehicles, efficient public transport and energy efficiency.
5. Promote ''Made in Bhutan'' products and encourage people to buy local.
6. Attract foreign direct investment in export-oriented industries such as ICT and agro-processing.
7. Use trade agreements to gain better market access and improve quality standards and certification for exports.
8. Develop service exports such as tourism, education and IT services.
These measures will narrow the deficit, protect foreign currency reserves and make the economy more self-reliant.', null, '[{"point": "Definition: difference between the value of a country''s exports and imports of goods (visible trade) over a period; surplus if exports exceed imports, deficit if imports exceed exports; part of the current account of the balance of payments", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Causes of Bhutan''s deficit: dependence on imports of fuel, food, vehicles, machinery, construction materials; narrow export base (electricity, ferro-alloys, minerals, agricultural products); hydropower construction imports", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Interventions: increase exports (hydropower, value-added minerals, agricultural and processed goods, new markets), import substitution (food self-sufficiency, local manufacturing), restrict/ tax non-essential and luxury imports (e.g. vehicles), reduce fuel imports (EVs, public transport), promote ''Made in Bhutan'', trade agreements and market access, FDI in export industries, quality standards", "marks": 12}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'trade-and-the-balance-of-payments'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 20,
    2160, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (General): BCSE 2021, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2021 Main Examination, General category, Paper III", "year": 2021, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, null, null, null, null, null, null)
